.TH DDCCONTROL-SCANMONITOR 1 "September 22, 2026"
.SH NAME
ddccontrol-scanmonitor \- generate a monitor database XML file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ddccontrol-scanmonitor
.RI [ dev:/dev/i2c-N ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-db-path
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B ddccontrol-scanmonitor
.B \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ddccontrol-scanmonitor
creates an editable XML file for the ddccontrol monitor database from a connected
DDC/CI monitor. With no device argument, it selects the only detected monitor
automatically or asks which monitor to scan when several are available.
For unattended use with several monitors, specify a device explicitly.
.PP
The scanner always accesses
.B /dev/i2c-*
directly, with no environment variable needed. Read and write permission on the
device is required; use
.B sudo ddccontrol-scanmonitor
if necessary. It reads EDID, capabilities and supported controls without changing
control values.
.PP
The generated XML enables known controls whose values could be read.
Uncertain controls are left commented out for manual testing.
Control and value definitions come from the database's
.BR options.xml .
Some monitor quirks, special initialization and unadvertised controls still need
manual edits.
.PP
By default the output is named after the monitor's PnP ID, for example
.BR DEL1234.xml ,
in the current directory. Existing files are never overwritten.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B dev:/dev/i2c-N
Scan this device instead of choosing from detected monitors.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List detected DDC/CI monitors and their device paths, then exit without writing XML.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " FILE"
Write XML to this file. The file must not already exist.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-db-path " DIR"
Read control definitions from
.IR DIR/options.xml .
By default, use the database location configured at build time, usually
.I /usr/share/ddccontrol-db
or
.IR /usr/local/share/ddccontrol-db .
Use the same database with the preview application's
.B \-b
option.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the program version.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an XML file interactively:
.PP
.nf
ddccontrol-scanmonitor
.fi
.PP
Choose a monitor and output path explicitly:
.PP
.nf
ddccontrol-scanmonitor dev:/dev/i2c-4 \-\-output ./DEL1234.xml
.fi
.PP
Try the generated file directly (replace the filename with your monitor's ID):
.PP
.nf
DDCCONTROL_NO_DAEMON=1 gddccontrol \-\-monitor-file ./DEL1234.xml
DDCCONTROL_NO_DAEMON=1 ddccontrol \-\-monitor-file ./DEL1234.xml
.fi
.PP
Keep the filename PNPID.xml and relaunch the application after editing it.
No system database changes or service restart are needed for this preview.
Loading a local file requires
.B DDCCONTROL_NO_DAEMON=1
and permission to access the I2C device directly. If needed, prefix the command
with \fBsudo env\fP.
.PP
Install a tested result into a typical package database and reload the service:
.PP
.nf
sudo cp \-i DEL1234.xml /usr/share/ddccontrol-db/monitor/DEL1234.xml
sudo systemctl restart ddccontrol.service
.fi
.PP
Use the database path printed by the scanner for your installation.
Test the enabled controls with
.BR gddccontrol (1)
or
.BR ddccontrol (1),
edit the XML as needed, and relaunch with
.B \-\-monitor-file
after each change.
Submit the tested file in a pull request to the ddccontrol-db repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A nonzero exit status indicates invalid arguments,
discovery or communication failure, an invalid database, or an output error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ddccontrol (1),
.BR gddccontrol (1)
.PP
Monitor database contribution guide:
.br
https://github.com/ddccontrol/ddccontrol-db/blob/master/doc/how-to-add-a-monitor.md
